able complex of churches and monasteries carved directly into the rock of the Azat River Gorge in modern-day Armenia, has been an active religious site since its founding in 1215. It stands as a testament to the architectural ingenuity of the medieval Armenian Church and reflects the broader cultural and spiritual currents of the time. Notably, it is associated with the Georgians and Armenians, both of whom played crucial roles in the region's Christian heritage. Its UNESCO World Heritage designation underscores its importance in the history of rock-hewn architecture, making it a cherished symbol of Armenian identity and heritage.

